WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.988
Formative and Summative Evaluation.

2
00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:07.406
ID 553 - Module 7: Evaluation.

3
00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:14.242
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:17.717
Evaluation answers two different questions.

5
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:28.550
Formative evaluation asks how the instruction can be
improved before it's finished; it happens during design and
development, and its audience is the design team.

6
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:42.057
Summative evaluation asks whether the finished instruction
worked and whether it's worth continuing; it happens after
implementation, and its audience is the people who fund and
mandate the instruction.

7
00:00:42.057 --> 00:00:43.929
Both are part of the design.

8
00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:50.615
Dick and Carey give each its own component and make revision
a loop that formative evaluation feeds.

9
00:00:51.215 --> 00:01:02.462
Let me walk you through a formative evaluation of a real
module: a twenty-five-minute e-learning module on barcode
medication administration with four objectives.

10
00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:06.211
Objective three is the decision about mismatch alerts.

11
00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:10.793
It was built from a storyboard and had never been seen by a
nurse.

12
00:01:10.793 --> 00:01:20.235
The plan was Dick and Carey's three stages: one-to-one
evaluation with three nurses, then a small group, then a
field trial on one unit.

13
00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:27.286
One-to-one: three nurses, one at a time, chosen to span the
range of the population.

14
00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:35.580
One had used the vendor's system elsewhere, one was typical,
one described herself as not a computer person.

15
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:45.333
I sat beside each of them, asked them to think aloud, noted
errors and confusions and pacing, and gave the posttest at
the end.

16
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:50.402
Findings: the alert screenshot was from an older software
version.

17
00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:57.698
The override step used the abbreviation MRN with no
expansion, and two of three didn't know it.

18
00:01:57.698 --> 00:02:03.918
And the anxious nurse took forty-one minutes for a module
planned at twenty-five.

19
00:02:03.918 --> 00:02:10.677
This stage catches the gross errors: wrong content, unclear
instructions, missing steps.

20
00:02:11.277 --> 00:02:20.726
After revising, I ran a small group: twelve nurses under
conditions close to real use, with me observing but not
intervening.

21
00:02:20.726 --> 00:02:28.058
I collected a pretest, a posttest, item-level results, time
on task, and a short attitude survey.

22
00:02:28.058 --> 00:02:35.542
Objectives one, two, and four came in between eighty-five
and one hundred percent posttest success.

23
00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:39.095
Objective three came in at fifty-eight percent.

24
00:02:39.095 --> 00:02:50.585
The item-by-objective table made the cause obvious: learners
had seen a single alert example and had no practice making
the decision under any pressure.

25
00:02:50.585 --> 00:02:57.388
The revision was two practice scenarios with feedback,
inserted before the posttest items.

26
00:02:57.388 --> 00:02:59.959
The overall score had looked fine.

27
00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:03.663
Analyzing by objective is what found the problem.

28
00:03:04.263 --> 00:03:13.405
Then the field trial: the revised module deployed to one
full unit, thirty-four nurses, through the LMS, with me
absent.

29
00:03:13.405 --> 00:03:19.957
Same data as the small group, plus feedback from the unit
educator who facilitated it.

30
00:03:19.957 --> 00:03:23.385
Objective three rose to eighty-eight percent.

31
00:03:23.385 --> 00:03:37.327
And a new problem appeared that no earlier stage could have
found: the LMS launched the module in a window too small for
the simulation, so learners scrolled inside a scrolling
frame.

32
00:03:37.327 --> 00:03:41.061
A technical fix, invisible until real conditions.

33
00:03:41.061 --> 00:03:52.565
That's what the field trial is for: it tests implementation,
the facilitator guides, the logistics, the technology, and
the effect of the real context.

34
00:03:53.165 --> 00:04:06.679
Alongside the learner-based stages, run expert reviews: a
subject-matter expert for accuracy, a second designer for
alignment and strategy, an accessibility reviewer for
conformance.

35
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:11.060
Expert review is fast and finds things learners can't name.

36
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:13.065
Then use the data properly.

37
00:04:13.065 --> 00:04:15.515
Build an item-by-objective table.

38
00:04:15.515 --> 00:04:24.945
Compare pretest and posttest by objective, not only overall,
because a high overall gain can hide one objective nobody
learned.

39
00:04:24.945 --> 00:04:34.672
Low success with adequate instruction time points to a
strategy problem; low success with little time points to a
coverage problem.

40
00:04:34.672 --> 00:04:42.321
Pair every problem with a location in the instruction and a
proposed revision, and keep a revision log.

41
00:04:42.921 --> 00:04:52.148
Summative evaluation judges the instruction against its
purpose, and the classic framework is Kirkpatrick's four
levels.

42
00:04:52.148 --> 00:04:57.376
Reaction: did learners find it engaging, relevant, and
satisfactory?

43
00:04:57.376 --> 00:05:03.912
Learning: did they acquire the intended knowledge, skills,
attitudes, and confidence?

44
00:05:03.912 --> 00:05:13.446
Behavior: do they apply it on the job, measured by
observation, supervisor reports, or system data at thirty to
ninety days?

45
00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:19.752
Results: did the targeted outcomes change, such as error
rates or help desk calls?

46
00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:20.751
Two cautions.

47
00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:29.978
Level one alone tells you almost nothing about
effectiveness, because satisfaction and learning are only
weakly related.

48
00:05:29.978 --> 00:05:42.281
And level four outcomes are affected by many things besides
instruction, so claim contribution rather than sole cause,
and design in a baseline or a comparison.

49
00:05:42.881 --> 00:05:47.745
For your project, the evaluation plan should specify four
things.

50
00:05:47.745 --> 00:05:57.699
Formative: which stages you'll run, with participants,
instruments, data, and the decision rules you'll use to
decide what to revise.

51
00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:10.272
Summative: the levels you'll measure and why, the
instruments and timing, the baseline or comparison strategy,
and who receives the report and what decision it informs.

52
00:06:10.272 --> 00:06:16.708
Logistics and ethics: who does what, when, at what cost, and
what happens to the data.

53
00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:17.756
And reporting.

54
00:06:17.756 --> 00:06:28.757
One rule above all: an evaluation plan that can actually be
carried out with the resources available beats an ambitious
one that will never happen.

55
00:06:28.757 --> 00:06:38.711
Bring your draft to the peer review exchange, and remember
that peer review is itself an expert-review stage of
formative evaluation.
